         FORMAT CSV FILE AS EQUALLY SPACED TEXT TABLES or LaTeX TABLES 
                                    - and -
   CONTROL SIGNIFICANT FIGURES DISPLAYED on TABLE, COLUMN OR INDIVIDUAL VALUES


This is a simple Python text formatting project. After searching for
this capability for a little while, I decided to write my own as the
following requirements seemed to be unique:

* Create a table of numbers from a CSV file
* Control the number of significant figures (significant digits) displayed
* Format numbers with “,” and ” ” like this 123,456.789 123
* Align columns on decimal points–this is best for readability
* Fit the columns automatically based on data and formatting

There are many code snippets that do one part or another of this, but 
I didn’t find anything that fulfilled all the requirements. So 
I created couple of simple classes to accomplish the task. One 
caveat is that the output needs to be displayed in a constant-space font.


Text table output is the default behavior.  Use -l to create LaTeX output.

Requires Python 3.11+.

Install / development
=====================

    pip install -e ".[dev]"     # installs the csv2table command
    pytest                      # runs tests with coverage (>= 85% enforced)
    ruff check . && ruff format --check . && mypy

Library use:

    from text_format_table import FormatTable
    print(FormatTable([["name", "value"], ["a", "1234.5"]], sf=3))

The examples below can be regenerated with:

    python -m text_format_table.demo

Examples
========

Custom s.f. per value

      column 1       |        column 2         |       column C    |    another column    
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- 
     1,000.          |       1,200.            |      1,230.       |        123.          
       123.2         |       1,000.            |        123.2      |      1,234.          
     1,234.2         |      -1,234.2           |      1,234.23     |      1,234.234       
     1,234.234 00    |           0.2           |          0.23     |         -0.23        
         0.235       |           0.234 6       |          0.234 57 |          0.002 35    
         0.000 234 6 |           0.000 023 457 | 23,000,000.       | 23,460,000.          
23,456,800.          | -23,456,800.            | 23,456,789.       | 23,456,789.123 345 0 
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- 

*****
Constant 3 s.f. (csv2table.py uses default 4, e.g. cat test.csv | ./csv2table.py -s3)

      column 1     |        column 2       |       column C |    another column 
------------------------------------------------------------------------------- 
     1,230.        |       1,230.          |      1,230.    |        123.       
       123.        |       1,230.          |        123.    |      1,230.       
     1,230.        |      -1,230.          |      1,230.    |      1,230.       
     1,230.        |           0.235       |          0.235 |         -0.235    
         0.235     |           0.235       |          0.235 |          0.002 35 
         0.000 235 |           0.000 023 5 | 23,500,000.    | 23,500,000.       
23,500,000.        | -23,500,000.          | 23,500,000.    | 23,500,000.       
------------------------------------------------------------------------------- 

*****
latex table output requires siunitx package:


%% Table generated by formatTable utility %%
%% add \usepackage[group-separator={,}]{siunitx}
%%
\begin{table}
\begin{tabular}{|S|S|S|S|}
\hline
   {column 1}    &     {column 2}      &    {column C} & {another column} \\
\hline
    1230.        &      1230.          &     1230.     &      123.        \\
     123.        &      1230.          &      123.     &     1230.        \\
    1230.        &     -1230.          &     1230.     &     1230.        \\
    1230.        &         0.235       &        0.235  &       -0.235     \\
       0.235     &         0.235       &        0.235  &        0.002 35  \\
       0.000 235 &         0.000 023 5 & 23500000.     & 23500000.        \\
23500000.        & -23500000.          & 23500000.     & 23500000.        \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}
%% Table generated by formatTable utility %%


For additional features and help, type

> csv2table -h        (or ./csv2table.py -h)
usage: csv2table [-h] [-l] [-s SF] [-c SFLIST] [-d DELIM] [-w MAX_COL_WIDTH]
                 [--config CONFIG]
                 [files ...]

positional arguments:
  files                 Input files (default: stdin).

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  -l, --latex           Generate LaTeX table (uses siunitx).
  -s, --sig-figs SF     Significant figures (default is 4).
  -c, --sig-figs-by-column SFLIST
                        Significant figures by column as list e.g.
                        "[1,2,2,2,4]"
  -d, --delimiter DELIM
                        Input file delimiter (default is comma)
  -w, --max-column-width MAX_COL_WIDTH
                        Maximum column width in characters (default is 50
                        char)
  --config CONFIG       TOML file overriding separators, defaults and paging.

Configuration
=============

Separators, default significant figures, max column width and rows per page
can be set in a TOML file passed with --config (command line flags win):

    default_sig_figs = 4
    max_column_width = 50
    rows_per_page = 44

    [number]
    int_separator = ","
    mantissa_separator = " "
    decimal_separator = "."
    digit_group_size = 3
    ellipsis = "..."

Changes in 2.0
==============

* Python 3 port. Output is byte-identical to 1.x (ties still round away
  from zero, as Python 2 did).
* Code moved to the text_format_table package; FNumber.py, FormatTable.py
  and csv2table.py remain as compatibility entry points.
* -c no longer evaluates its argument as Python; it accepts a list of
  integers such as "[1,2,2,4]" or "1,2,2,4".
* -w now works when given on the command line (it was previously ignored).
* Errors raise exceptions (and the CLI exits with status 2) instead of
  exiting with status 0 or printing a warning and producing misaligned
  output: significant figures <= 0 for a number, a row with more columns
  than the -c list (previously those columns were silently dropped), an
  empty table, or a max column width shorter than the ellipsis.
* camelCase methods getOffset/getRightOffset/getPadded are deprecated in
  favor of offset/right_offset/pad(); keyword arguments are snake_case
  (max_col_width).



